#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:79
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kappname; is a simple one player strategy game played against the computer. "
"The playing field is an 8 by 8 square board divided into 64 squares. The "
"game pieces used are two sided stones, where each side has its own, "
"distinctive color. If a piece is captured by an opposing player, that piece "
"is turned over to reveal the color of that player. A winner is declared when "
"one player has more pieces of his own color on the board and there are no "
"more possible moves."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:88
#, no-c-format
msgid "How to Play"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:90
#, no-c-format
msgid "Objective:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:90
#, no-c-format
msgid "Get control over the majority of squares on the board."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:92
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kappname; loads directly into the game play mode. As a first player you "
"have the right to move first."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:94 index.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid "Note:"
msgstr "Nòta :"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:94
#, no-c-format
msgid "By default the human player always has a right to the first move."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:95
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Now, imagine yourself that with each move your stone has to hop (jump) over "
"the enemy's one (or ones, as there might be more then one stone). Yet, your "
"stone does not relocate after the move, instead a new stone is placed on the "
"board onto where your stone would have appeared if it indeed could jump. "
"Once the move is done, all the enemy stones that you jump over, become yours "
"and change their color respectively."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:96
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Every single move you do in &kappname; has to be done in this manner. And "
"you can jump your stones vertically, horizontally and diagonally. But, in "
"case there is nowhere to jump, your turn will automatically skip."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The same rule applies to your enemy. If there is no possibility to make a "
"move the turn is skipped."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:98
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The idea here is to fill the board with the stones of your own color. The "
"player who manages to conquer the most of the game board is announced the "
"winner."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:102
#, no-c-format
msgid "Game Rules, Strategies and Tips"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:105
#, no-c-format
msgid "Game Basics"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:107
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A move consists of outflanking your opponents stone(s), then flipping the "
"outflanked stone(s) to reveal your color. A move is performed by placing the "
"mouse pointer over the desired square then &LMB; click."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:111
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To outflank means to place a stone on the board so that your opponent's row"
"(s) of stone(s) is bordered at each end by a stone of your color. A row may "
"be made up of one or more stones."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:115
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The game starts with each player having two stones automatically placed in "
"the center four squares of the board in the following pattern:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:119
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>&kappname; opening position</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:122
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>&kappname; opening position</phrase>"
msgstr ""

